/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula \n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{7}=5$ and ${a}_{4}=135.$ Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{5}{135} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-a5c77383-51aa-56e3-a7b4-492c5c358798" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c"] ["answer-fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/id #uuid "fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7"] ["answer-fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["answer-f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2" :answer/id #uuid "f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2"] ["answer-f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["answer-172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b" :answer/id #uuid "172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b"] ["answer-172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["answer-cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032" :answer/id #uuid "cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032"] ["answer-cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["answer-a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf" :answer/id #uuid "a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf"] ["answer-a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/correct "answer-fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/choices "answer-fae193f4-9c23-5527-acbb-523664fd50e7"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/choices "answer-f4430166-3747-5209-944a-ba1d986e72e2"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/choices "answer-172dbd89-674a-5726-9630-7750a809157b"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/choices "answer-cb86a186-51eb-54d5-a27b-6e2065c66032"] ["answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c" :answer-field/choices "answer-a6329dad-6c6c-5def-8fa9-b82fbf186adf"] ["question-a5c77383-51aa-56e3-a7b4-492c5c358798" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-bdd707aa-f658-5dc0-88e8-2cc1aff0bd8c"] [17592186147462 :knowledge-point/questions "question-a5c77383-51aa-56e3-a7b4-492c5c358798"] ["question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/id #uuid "e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6"] ["question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-63999"] ["question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/problem "The second term of a geometric sequence is $3$ and its fourth term is $27.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive."] ["question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{2}=3.$ Substituting $n=4$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{27}{3} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b"] ["answer-fd3e4f46-d7a4-5d08-926a-46332f200d82" :answer/id #uuid "fd3e4f46-d7a4-5d08-926a-46332f200d82"] ["answer-fd3e4f46-d7a4-5d08-926a-46332f200d82" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-fd3e4f46-d7a4-5d08-926a-46332f200d82" :answer/value "2"] ["answer-d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3" :answer/id #uuid "d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3"] ["answer-d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3" :answer/value "3"] ["answer-82fdc835-0849-5692-9878-06b5cdcfa034" :answer/id #uuid "82fdc835-0849-5692-9878-06b5cdcfa034"] ["answer-82fdc835-0849-5692-9878-06b5cdcfa034" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-82fdc835-0849-5692-9878-06b5cdcfa034" :answer/value "4"] ["answer-9ae7a74d-b582-5f2c-bdae-4e37c229e899" :answer/id #uuid "9ae7a74d-b582-5f2c-bdae-4e37c229e899"] ["answer-9ae7a74d-b582-5f2c-bdae-4e37c229e899" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9ae7a74d-b582-5f2c-bdae-4e37c229e899" :answer/value "6"] ["answer-6486fb05-2c8e-59e9-a3f2-53960b0abd36" :answer/id #uuid "6486fb05-2c8e-59e9-a3f2-53960b0abd36"] ["answer-6486fb05-2c8e-59e9-a3f2-53960b0abd36" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-6486fb05-2c8e-59e9-a3f2-53960b0abd36" :answer/value "9"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/correct "answer-d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/choices "answer-fd3e4f46-d7a4-5d08-926a-46332f200d82"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/choices "answer-d001064d-a7b9-5252-ae9d-6a2f9bf8b5d3"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/choices "answer-82fdc835-0849-5692-9878-06b5cdcfa034"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/choices "answer-9ae7a74d-b582-5f2c-bdae-4e37c229e899"] ["answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b" :answer-field/choices "answer-6486fb05-2c8e-59e9-a3f2-53960b0abd36"] ["question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-7642a863-35ca-5b78-ad8e-1abd8f1a804b"] [17592186147463 :knowledge-point/questions "question-e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6"] ["question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/id #uuid "5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5"] ["question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-250943"] ["question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/problem "Find the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose tenth term is $-8$ and whose twelfth term is $-72.$ You're given that the common ratio is negative.\n\n$r=${{field-1}}"] ["question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{12}=-72$ and ${a}_{10}=-8.$ Substituting $n=12$ and $m=10$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{12-10} & =\\frac{{a}_{12}}{{a}_{10}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{(-72)}{(-8)} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is negative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(-3)^{2} \\\\ r & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e"] ["answer-247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5" :answer/id #uuid "247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5"] ["answer-247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5" :answer/value "-3"] ["answer-field-f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e" :answer-field/key "field-1"] ["answer-field-f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank] ["answer-field-f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e" :answer-field/correct "answer-247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5"] ["answer-field-f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e" :answer-field/choices "answer-247506a5-a604-532e-ae42-730a93dd18f5"] ["question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-f78565af-1ce0-5d78-bd24-bee276b6e56e"] [17592186147463 :knowledge-point/questions "question-5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5"] [17592186148692 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a *positive* geometric sequence where the second term is $4$ and the fourth term is $64.$  How do we find the common ratio $r?$\n\nThis is a little trickier than before because we're not given two consecutive terms, but let's try to figure it out. First, let's denote\n\n$$\na_2 = 4, \\qquad a_4 = 64.\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by:\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=2$ and $n=4$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{2-1}={a}_{1}r \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing that $a_2 = 4$ and $a_4 = 64$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}r & =4 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{3} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have a system of equations to solve. To solve this system, we *divide* the *second* equation above by the *first* equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}}{{a}_{1}r} & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{{a}_{1}}{r}^{3}}{\\cancel{{a}_{1}}r} & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{3}}{r} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r.$ Note that $16 = 4^2,$ and so we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since our sequence must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio $r=4.$"] [17592186148693 :tutorial/content "Finding the common ratio by solving a system of equations is quite a lot of work. Is there an easier way?\n\nIndeed there is. Given two terms of a geometric sequence $a_n$ and $a_m,$ the formula for $r$ is as follows:\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works. Suppose that we have a geometric sequence whose second term is $3$ and whose fifth term is $192.$ What is the common ratio?\n\nHere, we're given $a_5 = 192$ and $a_2 = 3.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{192}{3} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r$ by noting that $64 = 4^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =64 \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={4}^{3} \\\\ r & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the common ratio is $r=4.$ \n\nWhen dealing with an equation that is **cubic** in $r$ (i.e., contains an $r^3$), then there is only one possible answer, unlike the squared case ($r^2$) where we have to pick from either positive or negative."] [17592186147713 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0]] "retractions" [[17592186147230 :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192$."] [17592186147230 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}, \\\\\na_{9} &= a_{1}r^{9 - 1} = a_{1}r^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192$, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{a_{1}r^{3} = 6; a_{1}r^{8} = 192\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{8}}{r^{3}} &= 32 \\\\\nr^{5} &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147226 :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686$."] [17592186147226 :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2$. Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{7 - 4} &= \\frac{a_{7}}{a_{4}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{-686}{2} \\\\\nr^{3} &= -343\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= -343 \\\\\nr^{3} &= (-7)^{3} \\\\\nr &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147229 :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20$. Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive."] [17592186147229 :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 3} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{3}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{20}{80} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nr^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186148692 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a *positive* geometric sequence where the second term is $4$ and the fourth term is $64$. How do we find the common ratio $r$?\n\nThis is a little trickier than before because we're not given two consecutive terms, but let's try to figure it out. First, let's denote\n\n$$\na_2 = 4, \\qquad a_4 = 64\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by:\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=2$ and $n=4$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1}r^{2 - 1} = a_{1}r \\\\\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing that $a_2 = 4$ and $a_4 = 64$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1}r &= 4 \\\\\na_{1}r^{3} &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have a system of equations to solve. To solve this system, we *divide* the *second* equation above by the *first* equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{3}}{a_{1}r} &= \\frac{64}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{3}}{a_{1}r} &= \\frac{64}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{3}}{r} &= 16 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r$. Note that $16 = 4^2$, and so we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nr^{2} &= 4^{2} \\\\\nr &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since our sequence must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the common ratio $r=4$."] [17592186148693 :tutorial/content "Finding the common ratio by solving a system of equations is quite a lot of work. Is there an easier way?\n\nIndeed there is. Given two terms of a geometric sequence $a_n$ and $a_m$, the formula for $r$ is as follows:\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate how this works. Suppose that we have a geometric sequence whose second term is $3$ and whose fifth term is $192$. What is the common ratio?\n\nHere, we're given $a_5 = 192$ and $a_2 = 3$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 2} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{2}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{192}{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the above equation for $r$ by noting that $64 = 4^3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= 64 \\\\\nr^{3} &= 4^{3} \\\\\nr &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the common ratio is $r=4$.\n\nWhen dealing with an equation that is **cubic** in $r$ (i.e., contains an $r^3$), then there is only one possible answer, unlike the squared case ($r^2$) where we have to pick from either positive or negative."]]}]